# Chrome DevTools MCP setup - __PROJECT__

Inspect authenticated network traffic in __PROJECT__'s target app via `chrome-devtools-mcp`
(Google's official CDP MCP server, not Playwright), so real requests/responses can be
captured and turned into reproducible probe scripts.

## 1. Launch the dedicated Chrome instance

```bash
scripts/launch-chrome.sh
```

This opens Chrome with `--remote-debugging-port=__PORT__` and an isolated profile at
`~/.chrome-__PROJECT__-profile`. The profile persists cookies/local storage across runs.

## 2. Log in manually (first time only)

Complete username/password/2FA by hand in that Chrome window. Every later launch reuses
the isolated profile, so it opens already logged in.

## 3. Register the MCP server

Confirm `.mcp.json` at the project root has an entry like:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "chrome-devtools": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest", "--browserUrl", "http://localhost:__PORT__"]
    }
  }
}
```

Restart Claude Code so the tools appear (`list_pages`, `navigate_page`, `get_network_requests`,
`evaluate_script`, etc).

## 4. Find the right tab and navigate

- `list_pages` - confirm which open tab is the target app.
- `navigate_page` - go to the feature you want to inspect, if not already there.

## 5. Capture requests

Call `get_network_requests` filtered by the target domain while exercising the feature.
For each interesting request, record:

- Method and full URL (including query string).
- Sensitive headers: `Authorization`, `Cookie`, `X-CSRF-Token`, and any other custom auth header.
- Request body.
- Response shape (status, key fields, pagination hints).

## 6. Turn a captured request into a probe script

For every request worth reproducing, write a minimal `scripts/probe/<name>.mjs` using plain
`fetch` with the same headers, to confirm it works without the browser before wrapping it as
an MCP tool under `src/`:

```js
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/some/endpoint", {
  method: "GET",
  headers: {
    Authorization: process.env.PROBE_AUTH_HEADER,
    Cookie: process.env.PROBE_COOKIE,
  },
});
console.log(res.status, await res.json());
```

Read captured secrets from environment variables or a local, gitignored file - never
hardcode them into the probe script.

## Security notes

- The CDP port (`__PORT__`) has no authentication of its own. It is safe only because it
  binds to localhost - never expose it via a tunnel or forward it to a public interface.
- `~/.chrome-__PROJECT__-profile` holds real session cookies. It lives outside the repo;
  never commit it, and never sync it through Dropbox/iCloud/etc.
- No token, cookie, or captured response body may enter the repo - not in probe scripts,
  not in commit messages, not in this doc.
